# Active Guix preparation recipe

KIT2 reuses the unchanged GNU Guix System 1.5.0 clean installation. The release
and verified ISO match the preceding laboratory, as recorded in
`C1/releases.tsv` and the preceding `C1/fix-c1-2/releases.tsv`. The prior recipe
is preserved here as `original-install.sh` and `original-config.scm`.

The original installation already copied to `install/guix/original.qcow2` is
compared byte-for-byte with the preceding read-only base, then kept read-only.
`kit2-reuse.sh` registers that image using `kit2-base.json`. Run it from the
laboratory root. It intentionally fails if the base link already exists.
The previous laboratory remains read-only.

The active transport is serial, with the existing `user` account and its
original `cal-test` password. The task explicitly permits serial when the
recipe cannot enable SSH. No lab public key is claimed to be installed:
previous SSH reconfiguration did not complete. `config.scm`, `access.sh` and
the recovery scripts remain records of unsuccessful prior preparations;
they are not applied in KIT2. No package, service, account or file inventory
is performed.

The original declaration explicitly requests the serial agetty. Primary
manual source: `C1/raw/guix/manual.texi`, agetty-service-type reference,
https://guix.gnu.org/manual/en/html_node/Base-Services.html . It states: “Type of the service that runs agetty, which implements virtual and
serial console log-in.” Existing serial access therefore
needs no system declaration change or reconfiguration.

The selected preceding runner's passive boot/login behavior is restored,
gated by `passive_boot_login: true` in this base's registry. It does not send
Ctrl-C while waiting for the first login prompt. See `kit2-passive.patch`
and preceding `C1/fix-c1-1/LAB.md`. Normal command framing remains unchanged.
Serial bytes are used only transiently for login and command-return framing;
no console tail or command output is saved during preparation.

`kit2-smoke.sh` creates one disposable clone, confirms boot/login and exact
script exit statuses, then shuts down and destroys it. QEMU runs under the
runner's persistent user service and holds one numbered slot lock. All
installer and guest-command output is discarded, including redirection inside
the test payloads. Only exit statuses are retained.
